## Phase 1: Setup Logdy Central Receiver (Alpine LXC)

Logdy serves as your central high-performance ring buffer. Because Alpine uses `musl` instead of `glibc`, ensure compatibility layers are installed first.

### 1. Fix Alpine Compatibility & Install Logdy
Download your preferred Logdy release and place it at `/usr/local/bin/logdy`.

```bash
# Add compatibility layers for standard linux binaries
apk update
apk add gcompat

# Ensure Logdy is executable
chmod +x /usr/local/bin/logdy
```

### 2. Configure OpenRC Init Script
Create a background-safe service initialization script:

```bash
nano /etc/init.d/logdy
```

Paste the following service configuration:

```sh
#!/sbin/openrc-run

name="logdy"
description="Logdy Real-time Centralized Log Server"
command="/usr/local/bin/logdy"

# Arguments open TCP 8123 for Vector, and UI 8080 for browsers. No-analytics prevents WAN overhead.
command_args="socket 8123 --port 8080 --ui-ip 0.0.0.0 --no-analytics --no-updates"

command_background="yes"
pidfile="/run/${RC_SVCNAME}.pid"
output_log="/var/log/logdy.log"
error_log="/var/log/logdy.err"

depend() {
    need net
}
```

### 3. Launch Logdy
```bash
chmod +x /etc/init.d/logdy
rc-update add logdy default
rc-service logdy start
```
Verify the interface is accessible via browser at `http://<YOUR_ALPINE_LOGDY_IP>:8080`.

