## Phase 3: Setup Dynamic PID Mapping (Proxmox Host)

Proxmox root directories are namespace-isolated and their physical process IDs (PIDs) shift when containers restart. To ensure a permanently valid connection to your nested Docker container, we map a persistent symbolic link.

### 1. Create the Symlink Automation Script
```bash
nano /usr/local/bin/update-lxc-docker-sock.sh
```

Paste this auto-resolving script:

```bash
#!/bin/bash
VMID="101" # Target your Docker container's LXC VMID here
LINK_PATH="/run/docker-nested-101.sock"

# Dynamic lookup of active host PID
PID=$(lxc-info -n $VMID -p -H 2>/dev/null)

if [ ! -z "$PID" ] && [ "$PID" != "-1" ]; then
    rm -f "$LINK_PATH"
    # Establish direct path to the nested namespace's run directory
    ln -s "/proc/$PID/root/run/docker.sock" "$LINK_PATH"
    echo "Successfully mapped nested Docker socket of LXC $VMID (PID $PID) to $LINK_PATH"
else
    echo "LXC $VMID is currently stopped."
    exit 1
fi
```

```bash
chmod +x /usr/local/bin/update-lxc-docker-sock.sh
```

### 2. Configure Systemd Service Overrides
Vector must run as `root` on the host to parse external namespace PIDs, and must execute the symlink updater *before* attempting configuration validation.

Force write your systemd drop-in configuration:

```bash
mkdir -p /etc/systemd/system/vector.service.d/

cat << 'EOF' > /etc/systemd/system/vector.service.d/override.conf
[Service]
User=root
Group=root

# Empty the existing ExecStartPre list to clear default validation ordering
ExecStartPre=

# 1. Update/Generate the nested socket symlink
ExecStartPre=/usr/local/bin/update-lxc-docker-sock.sh

# 2. Perform the Vector configuration validation check
ExecStartPre=/usr/bin/vector validate
EOF

systemctl daemon-reload
```

